WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.039
When you're setting up Cloud Code on a real project, how you onboard it really matters.

2
00:00:05.599 --> 00:00:11.600
For this video, we'll look at how it discovers project structure, what the slash init command

3
00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:18.079
generates, why the output needs review before team adoption, and what to verify before running

4
00:00:18.079 --> 00:00:24.000
Cloud Code on real production code. Let's get into it. Onboarding starts with knowing what

5
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:30.239
you're onboarding. Cloud Code is Anthropx's official agentic CLI. It's not a chat interface,

6
00:00:30.239 --> 00:00:37.279
and it's not autocomplete. It operates as a loop of planning, tool use, and response until the task

7
00:00:37.279 --> 00:00:44.560
is complete. The slash init command is a built-in slash command that analyzes the current repo and

8
00:00:44.560 --> 00:00:50.799
auto generates a starter clod.md. The important framing is that it generates a starting point,

9
00:00:50.799 --> 00:00:57.759
not a final configuration. And clod.md is the markdown file that provides persistent instructions

10
00:00:57.759 --> 00:01:03.759
for a project. It's loaded at session start, and it counts against the context window. Brevity and

11
00:01:03.759 --> 00:01:10.319
precision matter because verbose clod.md files consume space that would otherwise go to code

12
00:01:10.319 --> 00:01:16.080
and tool output. Starting Cloud Code is straightforward. You run the clod command

13
00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:20.959
from the project root directory. The root matters because it determines the default

14
00:01:20.959 --> 00:01:26.400
scope of what Cloud Code can see. In a new project, it starts with minimal context and

15
00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:34.319
reads whatever files already exist. In an existing project, it picks up any clod.md files at startup,

16
00:01:34.319 --> 00:01:39.839
then reads the file tree and other files on demand as tasks require. There's no automatic

17
00:01:39.839 --> 00:01:45.040
full directory scan at startup, and you don't need to manually list files or describe the structure

18
00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:50.720
before you begin. But the quality of what Cloud Code finds depends entirely on what's in the

19
00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:57.199
project. A well-structured repo with a clear readme and accurate configs gives Cloud Code

20
00:01:57.199 --> 00:02:02.800
much more to work with than a repo that has none of those things. The starting directory is the

21
00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:09.360
boundary. Cloud Code actively reads the project to orient itself rather than waiting for you to

22
00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:15.440
describe it. It reads file trees to understand directory layout and how modules are organized.

23
00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:21.279
It reads readme files for project purpose, setup instructions, and usage conventions.

24
00:02:21.279 --> 00:02:29.199
It reads config files like package.json for node projects, pyproject.toml for Python,

25
00:02:29.199 --> 00:02:35.199
and .env examples for expected environment variables. This automatic discovery is useful,

26
00:02:35.199 --> 00:02:40.880
but it has limits. If the project has an outdated readme, incorrect configs, or no

27
00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:46.160
documentation at all, Cloud Code will still try to orient based on what's there, but it gets a

28
00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:51.919
less accurate picture. The value of good project documentation compounds when Cloud Code is in the

29
00:02:51.919 --> 00:02:59.919
loop. The slash init command is the fastest way to give Cloud Code standing context on an unfamiliar

30
00:02:59.919 --> 00:03:05.360
code base. You run it once inside the project, and it analyzes the repo, file structure,

31
00:03:05.360 --> 00:03:11.919
programming languages, frameworks, and likely entry points. From that analysis, it auto-generates a

32
00:03:11.919 --> 00:03:18.960
starter Cloud.md. The generated file includes the project name, key files and directories,

33
00:03:18.960 --> 00:03:25.119
the detected technology stack, and suggested behavioral conventions based on patterns it found

34
00:03:25.119 --> 00:03:31.440
in the code. The core benefit is that you're not starting from a blank Cloud.md and trying to

35
00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:37.600
remember everything worth documenting. The slash init draft captures the structure automatically

36
00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:42.320
and gives you something concrete to edit rather than something to create from scratch.

37
00:03:43.520 --> 00:03:48.960
Let's look at the four things slash init generates. First, the project name detected from

38
00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:55.919
the repo name, package config, or the readme header. Second, key files, entry points, main

39
00:03:55.919 --> 00:04:02.240
config files, and primary source directories that Cloud Code should know about. Third, detected

40
00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:08.639
languages and frameworks based on file extensions and dependency manifests. If your project uses

41
00:04:08.639 --> 00:04:16.000
Python and FastAPI, slash init will typically detect both. Fourth, suggested rules, initial

42
00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:21.200
coding conventions, and behavioral guidelines inferred from what it found. These might include

43
00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:26.720
test patterns, import conventions, or formatting preferences. All four of these are starting points

44
00:04:26.720 --> 00:04:32.160
derived from automated analysis. They're useful scaffolding, but the team needs to verify each

45
00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:39.040
section before the file is used in practice. The gap between slash init output and a finished

46
00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:46.320
Cloud.md is worth being explicit about. The slash init draft is broad and generic. It captures what's

47
00:04:46.320 --> 00:04:52.320
detectable from file analysis, but it can't infer team conventions that aren't written in the code,

48
00:04:52.320 --> 00:04:59.119
security constraints specific to the environment, or project rules that live only in team knowledge.

49
00:04:59.119 --> 00:05:04.000
The reviewed version adds those things and removes anything the auto scan got wrong

50
00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:10.559
or flagged as important when it isn't. The other critical difference is precision. Every line in

51
00:05:10.559 --> 00:05:17.920
Cloud.md costs context window space. A verbose draft that includes filler reduces the space

52
00:05:17.920 --> 00:05:24.239
available for actual code and tool output during sessions. The goal of refinement is a file that's

53
00:05:24.239 --> 00:05:29.600
accurate, concise, and genuinely useful rather than comprehensive for its own sake.

54
00:05:30.559 --> 00:05:37.119
Refining slash init output is a required step, not optional polish. Verify that the detected

55
00:05:37.119 --> 00:05:43.679
frameworks and languages match the actual stack. Auto detection can misread dependency files,

56
00:05:43.679 --> 00:05:50.320
especially in polyglot repos. Add explicit rules that the scan couldn't infer. Your branching

57
00:05:50.320 --> 00:05:57.279
policy, test coverage requirements, security sensitive file paths. Remove noise, slash init

58
00:05:57.440 --> 00:06:03.359
sometimes flags files that aren't actually important to day to day work, or includes

59
00:06:03.359 --> 00:06:09.359
generic boilerplate that adds length without adding clarity. The review step is also where

60
00:06:09.359 --> 00:06:17.200
you add anything domain specific that the code base doesn't make obvious. A Cloud.md that ships

61
00:06:17.200 --> 00:06:23.600
as the raw slash init output is a liability because it may contain inaccuracies and will

62
00:06:23.600 --> 00:06:30.000
almost certainly miss critical context. Before running Cloud code on a real code base,

63
00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:36.559
three checks prevent the most common failures. First, Cloud.md exists and is accurate,

64
00:06:36.559 --> 00:06:42.000
meaning not the raw slash init draft, but a reviewed version that reflects the actual project.

65
00:06:42.720 --> 00:06:49.760
Second, permissions deny rules are configured in your settings dot json file. A deny rule on read

66
00:06:49.760 --> 00:06:56.239
of your dot nv files, secrets, and large binaries stops Cloud code from ever reading them into

67
00:06:56.239 --> 00:07:03.040
context. So, sensitive paths aren't exposed and the window isn't wasted. Third, entry points,

68
00:07:03.040 --> 00:07:10.239
test commands, and build steps are documented in Cloud.md. So, Cloud code knows how to interact

69
00:07:10.239 --> 00:07:16.799
with the project programmatically. These aren't bureaucratic check boxes. Each one directly

70
00:07:16.799 --> 00:07:22.480
prevents a real failure mode, bad context, exposed credentials, or an agent that can't

71
00:07:22.480 --> 00:07:31.200
run the project it's working on. The bad version of this shows up quietly. A generated Cloud.md

72
00:07:31.200 --> 00:07:37.119
guesses the stack wrong. Nobody reads it and every session after that starts from a false premise.

73
00:07:37.920 --> 00:07:44.000
That's why the scaffold from slash init is a draft and not a deliverable. It can read your

74
00:07:44.000 --> 00:07:50.559
file tree, but it can't read the conversations your team never wrote down. You supply those.

75
00:07:51.119 --> 00:07:54.320
And that brings this one to a close. I'll see you in the next one.

